bound the destination of a span, and name the surface it belongs to

Money Manager EX died every time its save panel was opened and then took the keyboard, 4 runs in 5
before this. The fault was a store of 0xFF000000 into an unmapped page from
O2argb8u_copy_by_coverage, and the cause turned out to be in the Wayland backend rather than here,
so this is the instrument that found it plus the guards that make the same class of defect say
something instead of dying hundreds of instructions away from its cause.

WHAT THE THREE SPAN WRITERS NOW REFUSE.

O2ImageRead_BGRA8888_to_argb8u returns pixelBytes + y * bytesPerRow + x * 4 on little endian and
hands that straight back as the destination. scanlineAtY bounds the ROW and nothing bounds
x + length within it, so a span wider than the surface runs into the next row, and off the last row
it leaves the mapping entirely. Upstream cocotron knew: there is a viewport assert at the call site
in O2DContextFillEdgesOnSurface, disabled under an if 0. ciderClampSpan is that assert, awake, and
it reports rather than aborts.

A paint that reports more pixels than it was asked for is the second way out. Every one of these
loops does length -= chunk and breaks on length == 0, so one oversized chunk drives length negative
and the test never fires again while src and dst walk forever. ciderClampChunk ends that, and a
chunk of zero, which spins instead, with it.

ciderDirectIsSane is the third and it is the one that mattered: the direct pointer must lie inside
the surface that produced it. It did, every time, which is what ruled out both of the above and
turned the search towards the surface itself.

WHAT THE TRACE ANSWERS, under CIDER_TRACE_SURFACE.

O2Surface prints itself as it is made, resized and freed, with its size, stride, pixel base and
whether it OWNS those pixels. The span writer prints one line per buffer it draws into, not per
span, with a backtrace under it. The process dies mid draw, so the last buffer named is the one it
died in and the backtrace beneath it is the draw that reached for it. For #214 that read:

  CIDER_SURFACE new self=0x74c95f635640 500x173 row=2192 pixels=0x74c95d5efde0 owns=0
  CIDER_SPANDIRECT surface=0x74c95f635640 pixels=0x74c95d5efde0+379216 direct=0x74c95d604d7c
      x=107 y=39 len=1
  ...
  1 O2RasterizeWriteCoverageSpan8888_Copy
  6 NSRectFillListUsingOperation
  8 -[NSTextView drawInsertionPointInRect:color:turnedOn:]
  10 -[NSWindow becomeKeyWindow]
  12 cider_wayland_set_keyboard_focus

owns=0 was the answer: the window surface is built over the Wayland shm mapping and does not own
it, and release_backing had munmapped it. The fix is in src/darwin/wayland/window.rs.

A PROBE THAT COULD NOT BE USED, recorded so it is not tried again. Nothing in memory can tell a
live page from a dead one when the object still describes it, so a syscall was tried: write to
/dev/null and stat of the pointer, either of which answers EFAULT on a real kernel. Run once
against 0x10 as a control, the guest DIED INSIDE THE PROBE. The syscall emulation does not fault
protect its copyin, so neither call can be used to ask a question about an address.
